  "DEBT_RECOVERY_GUIDANCE": {
    "CLOSED": "Close the case once the debt is paid, settled or written off, and reconcile the lot ledger.",
    "COMMITTEE_REVIEW": "The committee can decide to start proceedings to recover a liquidated debt from a lot owner (Standard Module s 52(1)(e)(i)). If a contribution has been outstanding for 2 years, the body corporate must start proceedings within 2 months after the end of the 2 years (Standard Module s 166(2)). The debt recovery page warns when a lot reaches this point. How the 2 years is counted is StrataVerze's reading: from the date for payment of the oldest unpaid contribution.",
    "ENFORCEMENT": "Enforce the order or judgment. Not confirmed: the enforcement options. Rely on the solicitor's advice.",
    "FINAL_NOTICE": "Tell the owner in writing that the body corporate may start proceedings to recover the unpaid contributions, the penalty and recovery costs reasonably incurred. Recovery may start once a contribution is unpaid by the date for payment. The research notes record no notice that must be given before proceedings, so this notice is StrataVerze practice.",
    "HARDSHIP_REVIEW": "If satisfied there are special reasons, the body corporate may waive the penalty or recovery costs, in whole or in part, or allow the discount for paying on time (Standard Module s 166(6)). There is no hardship statement and no statutory payment plan in Queensland. Treating a request as a step is StrataVerze practice. Not confirmed: whether the committee can decide a waiver or a general meeting resolution is needed.",
    "INITIAL_CONTACT": "Contact the owner informally about the unpaid contributions and the amount owing. Queensland law has no statutory payment plan and no hardship statement. The owner can ask the body corporate to waive the penalty or recovery costs for special reasons. While the lot owes the body corporate money, it can vote only on a resolution without dissent or a committee election, and its owner cannot be on the committee. This contact is StrataVerze practice.",
    "LEDGER_VERIFICATION": "Check each unpaid contribution was validly fixed: by ordinary resolution of the body corporate (Standard Module s 162(1), (2)), or for an interim contribution by the committee (Standard Module s 162(3), (4)). Check its contribution notice was given at least 30 days before the date for payment. Check payments were applied first to the penalty, then to the contribution, then to recovery costs (Standard Module s 166(5)). This check, and showing contributions, penalty and costs separately, is StrataVerze practice.",
    "LEGAL_REFERRAL": "Refer the matter to a solicitor, or prepare the claim, with the ledger, the contribution notices and the committee's decision. The owner when the debt became payable and a later owner, including a mortgagee in possession, are jointly and severally liable (Standard Module s 166(3), (4)). Recovery costs reasonably incurred are recoverable (Standard Module s 166(1)(c)). Government guidance gives legal fees and body corporate manager arrears notice fees as examples. Not confirmed: whether any particular cost beyond that, including a StrataVerze fee, is recoverable from the owner.",
    "PROCEEDINGS": "A claim to recover a debt of up to $25,000 is a minor civil dispute in QCAT. The body corporate may instead claim in a court. An adjudicator cannot decide a debt dispute (BCCMA s 229A). Once QCAT or court proceedings start, any dispute resolution process for the debt ends. Not confirmed: the Magistrates Court limit for debts over $25,000. Rely on the solicitor's advice.",
    "REMINDER_NOTICE": "Send a written reminder showing the unpaid contributions, any penalty and any recovery costs separately. Owners are shown penalty, never interest. No period for a reminder is set by law, so the building sets its own. This reminder is StrataVerze practice.",
