  "FOR_REVIEW": [
    {
      "before": "any debt workflow that automates authority to sue",
      "key": "S18_COMMITTEE_DELEGATION",
      "kind": "LEGAL",
      "point": "Whether a committee may authorise proceedings under s 18(2) or (2A) by its default delegation (s 11(5)), given s 11(3). Until settled, authority to sue needs a recorded general meeting resolution of the right kind.",
    },
    {
      "before": "the rate is used as a default cap or shown as the maximum",
      "key": "PENALTY_INTEREST_RATE",
      "kind": "LEGAL",
      "point": "Check the current Penalty Interest Rates Act maximum against the Government Gazette notice; the 10% figure is guidance only.",
    },
    {
      "before": "dispute workflows or notice text use the period",
      "key": "DISPUTE_MEETING_PERIOD",
      "kind": "LEGAL",
      "point": "Which of 14 days (approved form text) and 28 calendar days (OCR Sch 2 rule 7(5)) applies to the fee dispute meeting. The pack uses 28.",
    },
    {
      "before": "historical notices are checked against the form",
      "key": "APPROVED_FORMS_DATE",
      "kind": "LEGAL",
      "point": "The date the current approved fee notice forms applied from (the 28 April 2025 date is not confirmed). NOTICE_RULES.checked.form.appliedFrom has no value until it is (stage 2, PR 6a).",
    },
    {
      "before": "Victorian fee notices are generated",
      "key": "APPROVED_FORMS_USE",
      "kind": "STRATAVERZE",
      "point": "How the platform reproduces the approved fee and final fee notice forms, changing only layout and adding only reference numbers, manager details and web or email details. Since stage 2, PR 6a this blocks every fee notice: NOTICE_RULES requires the approved form (s 31(1)) and StrataVerze has no renderer for it, so each Victorian fee notice is refused with that reason.",
    },
    {
      "before": "any manager contract feature for Victorian buildings",
      "key": "MANAGER_CONTRACT_FORM",
      "kind": "LEGAL",
      "point": "Obtain the approved form for a manager's contract of appointment (s 119(3)).",
    },
    {
      "before": "sign-off",
      "key": "OWNERS_CORPORATION_TYPE_SUPPORTED",
      "kind": "STRATAVERZE",
      "point": "\`supported: true\` on the unlimited owners corporation option is a StrataVerze choice for this draft, not a rule of law. Limited owners corporations are listed but blocked.",
    },
    {
      "before": "sign-off",
      "key": "ADMINISTRATIVE_FUND",
      "kind": "STRATAVERZE",
      "point": "The administrative fund is a StrataVerze ledger choice (status STRATAVERZE): the Act names no such fund. Confirm the name owners see, or another. Since stage 2 PR 3 it is also marked required, as a ledger choice and not a rule of law, so every Victorian building is given it and has a ledger for its annual fees (the chart of accounts needs at least one fund). The screens show it as required with the note that this is a StrataVerze choice. Confirm.",
    },
    {
      "before": "Victorian buildings are set up",
      "key": "MAINTENANCE_FUND_REQUIRED",
      "kind": "STRATAVERZE",
      "point": "The maintenance fund's \`requiredWhen\` (stage 2, PR 3) reads the tier and whether a maintenance plan is approved (MAINTENANCE_PLAN_APPROVED, recorded by the adviser). (1) Tiers 1 and 2: required, whether or not a plan is approved yet. The notes say these tiers must prepare and approve a plan (s 36) and that the fund is required where a plan is approved (s 40), so requiring it from the start is a StrataVerze reading, chosen so a building is never given fewer funds than the law requires. (2) Tier 5: not required even with an approved plan, because 2-lot and services only owners corporations are exempt from the maintenance plan and maintenance fund provisions (s 7A(1)(b); s 8). (3) Tiers 3 and 4: required where a plan is approved, and not required otherwise. Whether a tier 3 or 4 owners corporation may keep the fund without an approved plan is not in the notes, so the screens say only that it is not required. (4) Where the tier cannot be worked out, or at tier 3 or 4 no plan answer is recorded, the fund is not worked out and the building is flagged. Confirm each choice.",
    },
    {
      "before": "notices, minutes or PDFs carry the name",
      "key": "BODY_CORPORATE_NAME",
      "kind": "STRATAVERZE",
      "point": "The name format is CAV's example only, and needs the owners corporation's number on the plan, which the shared name tokens do not cover. Also settle the capital letter at the start of a heading.",
    },
    {
      "before": "each pack release, and 25 Nov 2026",
      "key": "PENDING_COMMENCEMENT",
      "kind": "LEGAL",
      "point": "Check the Government Gazette for proclamations of CLAA 2026 Part 6 Div 2 and HSRA 2025 Part 4 Div 1 before each pack release. SR 53/2026 commences on 25 Nov 2026 and needs a new pack version to switch on.",
    },
    {
      "before": "any code reads a dated rule",
      "key": "DATED_RULES",
      "kind": "STRATAVERZE",
      "point": "Each pending change is restated in DATED_RULES. An enacted or made change stays off, even after its fixed or latest start date, until a pack version records the day it commenced; once that date passes the reader refuses the rule and the pending law check lists it (SR 53/2026 from 25 Nov 2026). In-force values start on the day the version read came into force (OCA Version 024, OCR Version 002), so earlier days have no value. Before s 32I the payment order is recorded as no statutory rule, NOT_CONFIRMED. The pending proxy limit carries the exceptions in force, NOT_CONFIRMED (the notes say only that the s 89D(2) exceptions are not amended). Confirm these choices.",
    },
    {
      "before": "25 Nov 2026",
      "key": "FIXED_DATE_COMMENCEMENT",
      "kind": "STRATAVERZE",
      "point": "Whether an instrument that fixes its own start date (SR 53/2026, 25 Nov 2026) should count as commenced on that date without a new pack version recording it. Until decided, it does not: from 25 Nov 2026 the reader refuses the manager registration fees until a pack version records commencedOn.",
    },
    {
      "before": "CLAA 2026 Part 6 Div 2 commences (1 Dec 2027 at the latest)",
      "key": "PAYMENT_PLAN_WORKFLOW",
      "kind": "STRATAVERZE",
      "point": "FEATURES.statutoryPaymentPlans is false: Victoria's payment plans are not in force, and the enacted scheme (owner-occupier refusal grounds, deemed acceptance, tiers 1 to 4) differs from the NSW workflow.",
    },
    {
      "before": "any fee in dollars is charged or shown",
      "key": "CALCULATED_DOLLARS",
      "kind": "LEGAL",
      "point": "The fee dollar table is the researcher's calculation. Check the Monetary Units Act rounding rules before any amount is charged or shown, and never show it as an official figure.",
    },
    {
      "before": "the pack moves to RULE_PACKS",
      "key": "DEFERRED_SHARED_SHAPES",
      "kind": "STRATAVERZE",
      "point": "No shared shape is deferred: LATE_CHARGE and PAYMENT_ALLOCATION_ORDER are given since stage 2, PR 5, NOTICE_RULES since stage 2, PR 6a, and MEETING_RULES since stage 2, PR 8a-i (DEFERRED_SHARED_SHAPES is empty).",
    },
    {
      "before": "Victorian meetings are run",
      "key": "MEETING_RULES",
      "kind": "STRATAVERZE",
      "point": "MEETING_RULES (stage 2, PR 8a-i) is StrataVerze's mapping of MEETINGS, RESOLUTIONS, ARREARS and RECORDS to the shared shape. StrataVerze choices: the names (Ordinary, Special, Unanimous and Interim Special Resolution); a general meeting and the motions list offer ordinary, special and unanimous resolutions, and a committee ordinary resolutions only; an interim special resolution is never put on an agenda; pre-meeting electronic voting is not offered; common property rows are not counted as lots; voting papers are kept 12 months as the secret ballot period; an item with no resolution category (such as an election) cannot be recorded as resolved at a general meeting, because every outcome there comes from the count and such an item has no test to count it by (it can be recorded as lost, or deferred). Confirm each.",
    },
    {
      "before": "Victorian meetings are run",
      "key": "MEETING_RULES_SPECIAL",
      "kind": "LEGAL",
      "point": "A special resolution counted at a meeting (a show of hands) needs 75% of all lots for, not of the votes cast (s 96). An owner in arrears may vote on it (s 89B), so the lots in arrears are counted for and in the total like any other. A poll is taken on an ordinary resolution only, so a special or unanimous resolution is never counted by lot entitlement at a meeting (s 96 counts it by lot entitlement "on a ballot or poll"; who may ask for a poll on it is not in the notes). A unanimous resolution needs every lot for (s 95). Confirm.",
    },
    {
      "before": "Victorian meetings are run",
      "key": "MEETING_RULES_INTERIM",
      "kind": "LEGAL",
      "point": "A special resolution that fails may pass as an interim special resolution (s 97): at least 50% of all lots for and not more than 25% of all lots against, or no vote against at a meeting with a quorum. The count says so, but an interim resolution is not recorded at the meeting until StrataVerze records interim resolutions, their notice and the 29-day petition period (stage 2, PR 8b); the chair is told and may defer the item. It is tested only from a special resolution item. How the first route is counted on a poll is NOT_CONFIRMED, so it is refused on a poll. StrataVerze reads the second route (s 97(1A), no vote against at a meeting with a quorum) as needing at least 1 vote for, so a meeting where nobody votes passes nothing (to confirm). Confirm.",
    },
    {
      "before": "Victorian meetings are run",
      "key": "MEETING_RULES_ORDINARY",
      "kind": "STRATAVERZE",
      "point": "An ordinary resolution counted at a meeting (since stage 2, PR 8a-ii, 29 Oct 2026) needs the chair's answer whether the meeting had a quorum for the item (MEETING_RULES_QUORUM). With "yes" it is recorded as carried or lost by the votes cast (by lot entitlement on a poll). With "no" a count that carries is an interim resolution (s 78), refused until interim resolutions are recorded (stage 2, PR 8b), and one that fails is recorded as lost. Not answered, it is refused. Equal votes are refused too: a chair who is a lot owner or a proxy has a casting vote and without it the resolution is not passed (s 89A), and StrataVerze does not record who chaired or a casting vote. A carried ordinary resolution can now authorise interest (LATE_CHARGE: any resolution carried at a general meeting, s 29(1)). Confirm.",
    },
    {
      "before": "Victorian meetings are run",
      "key": "MEETING_RULES_TIER_5",
      "kind": "LEGAL",
      "point": "Tier 5 owners corporations (2-lot subdivisions and services only) are exempt from the meeting and resolution rules in ss 7A and 8, and for a 2-lot owners corporation a special resolution means a unanimous one. Since stage 2, PR 8a-ii (29 Oct 2026) their votes are not counted at all: at a general meeting every outcome other than a deferral is refused for a tier 5 owners corporation, whatever the item (countingRefusedWhen, read from TIER), so the chair can only defer. Where the tier cannot be worked out (one of several owners corporations, mostly or only non-occupiable lots, 2 occupiable lots with other lots, a lot with no occupiable answer, the lots not matching the total, or the services only answer missing) every count is refused too, with what the building rules are missing: this tightens PR 8a-i, which recorded special and unanimous resolutions for such buildings. Counting a 2-lot special resolution as a unanimous one is not done (a StrataVerze choice, until the tier 5 rules are applied). With 2 lots or fewer a special resolution is still never tested as an interim special resolution (a second line of defence). Confirm.",
    },
    {
      "before": "Victorian meetings are run",
      "key": "MEETING_RULES_ARREARS",
      "kind": "LEGAL",
      "point": "An owner in arrears is read as a lot with a fee notice issued, past its due date and not paid in full on the meeting's date, as for Queensland. The rule that a payment other than cash counts only if made at least 4 business days before the vote (s 89B) is not applied. Since stage 2, PR 8a-ii (29 Oct 2026) the rule applies to tiers 1 to 4 only, as tier 5 is exempt from s 89B (s 7A): an owner of a tier 5 owners corporation is not barred from voting online on a motion. Where the tier cannot be worked out, the owner is barred as in tiers 1 to 4 (a StrataVerze choice: it fails closed). At a meeting both are moot, as neither is counted. Confirm.",
    },
    {
      "before": "Victorian meetings are run",
      "key": "MEETING_RULES_QUORUM",
      "kind": "LEGAL",
      "point": "Whether the meeting had a quorum (s 77: at least 50% of the lots, or if that is not available 50% of the total lot entitlement) is the chair's answer for each item, kept with the outcome in the meeting record with who recorded it (Matthew Wrigley's decision of 29 Oct 2026, DECISIONS). It is never filled in for the chair, and an item not answered is refused. StrataVerze does not record attendance yet, so it does not work the quorum out. It refuses a "no quorum" answer only where the lots whose votes are counted already make up half of all lots. Whether s 78 judges quorum once for the meeting or for each item is not in the notes (asked for each item, to confirm). When attendance is recorded (meeting management, Stage C) the quorum will be worked out from it instead. Confirm.",
    },
    {
      "before": "Victorian meetings are run",
      "key": "MEETING_RULES_NOTICE_AND_MINUTES",
      "kind": "STRATAVERZE",
      "point": "Committee meeting notice (3 business days, s 109) is not checked, as the shape holds whole days only, and no deadline for ordinary minutes is checked, as none was found. Confirm.",
    },
    {
      "before": "sign-off",
      "key": "GUIDANCE_CONFLICTS",
      "kind": "LEGAL",
      "point": "Confirm that the pack follows the Act wherever CAV guidance differs (GUIDANCE_CONFLICTS).",
    },
    {
      "before": "Victorian buildings are set up",
      "key": "FINANCIAL_YEAR_AND_MULTI_OC_TIERS",
      "kind": "LEGAL",
      "point": "The financial year (no statutory definition found) and the tier of an owners corporation on a plan with several.",
    },
    {
      "before": "any rule reads the tier",
      "key": "BUILDING_SELECTORS_TIER",
      "kind": "LEGAL",
      "point": "How TIER is worked out. (1) An owners corporation recorded as one of several on its plan is refused: the notes do not confirm how its tier is assessed (FINANCIAL_YEAR_AND_MULTI_OC_TIERS). (2) A services only owners corporation is tier 5 before s 7(7) and (8) are read, because tiers 1 to 4 exclude services only and s 7(7) applies tiers 1 and 2 only; that order is a StrataVerze reading. (3) Where s 7(7) or (8) applies (mostly or only non-occupiable lots) the tier is refused, because the notes do not say how the non-occupiable lots are counted with the occupiable ones. (4) A plan with 2 occupiable lots and no other lots is tier 5 (a 2-lot subdivision, "comprising of 2 occupiable lots"); with 2 occupiable lots and other lots it is refused until decided, because the notes do not say whether the other lots count. (5) Every tier is marked supported as a StrataVerze proposal.",
    },
    {
      "before": "Victorian buildings are set up",
      "key": "BUILDING_FACTS_RECORDED_BY",
      "point": "Who records each lot's occupiable answer, the services only answer, whether the owners corporation is the only one on its plan and whether a maintenance plan is approved: the adviser or StrataVerze, in the Building rules section, outside the locked building record. The maintenance plan answer is flagged only where the maintenance fund depends on it (tiers 3 and 4). The lot form and the data import do not ask for them yet, so a new lot is flagged until it is answered. A lot of StrataVerze's PARKING or STORAGE type answered occupiable is flagged and the tier refused, reading those types as the car park and storage locker of s 3 (a StrataVerze choice).",
    },
    {
      "before": "sign-off",
      "key": "ENTITLEMENT_DECIDES",
      "kind": "LEGAL",
      "point": "Which schedule decides each use (ENTITLEMENT_SCHEDULES \`decides\`, stage 2 PR 4), read from the notes: lot liability decides CONTRIBUTIONS (annual, special and upgrading fees, ss 23(3), 24(2), 53(1A)); lot entitlement decides POLL_VOTE (s 89(3)), SPECIAL_RESOLUTION and UNANIMOUS_RESOLUTION on a ballot or poll (ss 95, 96), QUORUM_FALLBACK only where 50% of the lots is not available (s 77: lots are counted first), COMMON_PROPERTY_SHARE (SA s 30) and INSURANCE_PREMIUM_FEE (s 23A, read by nothing until CONTRIBUTION_INSURANCE_S23A is decided). The cap on contributions (s 28(2)), administrator costs (s 175), the nomination by owners holding at least 25% of lot entitlements of the person who convenes a special general meeting or arranges a ballot (ss 74, 83 to 85) and VCAT consent (SA s 34D(3)(ba)) stay in \`usedFor\` with no key, as no feature reads them. A "benefits more pays more" fee (ss 23(3A), 24(2A)) is not shared by either schedule and is not offered. Confirm the mapping.",
    },
    {
      "before": "Victorian buildings are set up",
      "key": "SECOND_SCHEDULE_HELD",
      "kind": "STRATAVERZE",
      "point": "Each lot's lot entitlement is held in lots.lot_entitlement and its lot liability in lots.entitlement, each as a whole number of at least 1 (the shape of the columns). The notes do not say either is a whole number, so a register with fractions would need the columns changed. Lot entitlement is locked like lot liability: StrataVerze enters and changes it, on the lot form, the CSV lot import or the Add Property wizard. A lot without it is flagged in Building data missing, and anything that needs it is refused, never worked out from lot liability. Whether a 2-lot owners corporation, whose initial allocation needs no surveyor (SA s 27EA), always has a lot entitlement is not in the notes: it is flagged like any other. A change of the building's state clears each lot's lot entitlement. Confirm.",
    },
    {
      "before": "Victorian interest is charged",
      "key": "LATE_CHARGE",
      "kind": "LEGAL",
      "point": "LATE_CHARGE (stage 2, PR 5). No interest until a resolution at a general meeting authorises it, with its rate (s 29(1)), so a building starts with none. No rate can be recorded yet: the cap (the Penalty Interest Rates Act maximum, s 29(2)) has no value until PENALTY_INTEREST_RATE is checked, and 10% is never used as a default cap. Even with a rate, nothing is charged until a pack version records how interest accrues (simple or otherwise) and the days a year's rate is shared over (365, or another basis): the notes give neither. Interest runs on amounts outstanding after the due date with no grace period (s 29(1)). The lowest rate, 0, is a StrataVerze choice. Rounding (once, half up, to the cent, on the sum of the days, as NSW interest) and applying each payment to the fee first are StrataVerze choices. Once checked, the cap may need to be a dated rule (DATED_RULES), as the rate is fixed by notice. Confirm each.",
    },
    {
      "before": "Victorian interest is charged",
      "key": "INTEREST_WAIVER",
      "kind": "LEGAL",
      "point": "The waiver in a particular case (s 29(3)), recorded for one notice. Proposed, not decided: (1) who decides: the notes do not say, so decidedBy is NOT_CONFIRMED and no waiver can be recorded until it is decided; a general meeting resolution (the meeting and motion) is proposed, as the committee's power is not in the notes; (2) what may be waived: only all of the interest on the notice is offered (WHOLE); whether s 29(3) allows waiving part (an amount or the days of a period, which the engine and the table support) is not in the notes. The reasons are required, because decisions to waive or not, with reasons, are reported to the AGM (s 29(4)); the AGM report itself is not produced yet, and a decision not to waive is not recorded. A waiver cannot be changed or removed once recorded. A waiver is refused on a notice that already has interest charged, until a way to reverse a charge exists. Decide each.",
    },
    {
      "before": "sign-off",
      "key": "PAYMENT_ALLOCATION_ORDER",
      "kind": "STRATAVERZE",
      "point": "PAYMENT_ALLOCATION_ORDER: fees, then interest, then recovery costs, as NSW. Decided by Matthew Wrigley on 28 Oct 2026 as a StrataVerze choice (DECISIONS), since the notes record no order in force before s 32I. DATED_RULES.PAYMENT_ALLOCATION_ORDER still records no statutory order before s 32I; switching to s 32I by date is stage 2, PR 7.",
    },
    {
      "before": "the s 23A fee is applied to Victorian fees",
      "key": "CONTRIBUTION_INSURANCE_S23A",
      "kind": "LEGAL",
      "point": "The s 23A insurance premium fee is NOT applied yet: the pack has no CONTRIBUTION_INSURANCE, so the whole of every Victorian fee, insurance included, is shared by lot liability (s 23(3), CONFIRMED). The notes limit s 23A to the reinstatement and replacement premium (the s 59 cover), shared by lot entitlement. Public liability (s 60) and any other policy stay in annual fees by lot liability. To apply it, decide: (1) whether an owners corporation must raise the reinstatement premium as the s 23A fee, or may keep it in annual fees by lot liability (not in the notes); (2) whether the s 23A fee may be on the annual fee notice or needs a fee notice of its own (not in the notes); (3) how the reinstatement premium is held apart from the other policies, since the budget's Insurance account holds them together; (4) the StrataVerze choices that would come with it: insurance paid from the administrative fund, a budget with no reinstatement premium not refused (2-lot and services only owners corporations need not insure, ss 7A, 8, and others may insure another way, ss 61A, 63, 64), a special fee shared by lot liability only (s 24(2)), and no recovery from a culpable owner (s 23A) offered yet. The shared engine (src/lib/levies/contribution-shares.ts) is ready for INSURANCE_PREMIUM_FEE once these are decided.",
    },
    {
      "before": "Victorian fee notices are generated",
      "key": "NOTICE_RULES",
      "kind": "STRATAVERZE",
      "point": "NOTICE_RULES (stage 2, PR 6a). Every Victorian fee notice is refused today, each with its reason: the approved form (s 31(1); APPROVED_FORMS_USE), the obligation to pay within 28 days (s 31(2)(a)), the interest rate (s 31(2)(b): notices cannot state an annual-rate interest yet) and the dispute resolution process (s 31(2)(c)) are not on StrataVerze notices, and the most notice a fee notice may give is not confirmed. The 35-day lead is Matthew Wrigley's decision of 28 Oct 2026 (DECISIONS). Recorded here so it is visible at sign-off.",
    },
    {
      "before": "Victorian fee notices are generated",
      "key": "NOTICE_MINIMUM",
      "kind": "LEGAL",
      "point": "The 28-day least notice is StrataVerze's reading of s 31(2)(a) (the obligation to pay within 28 days after the date of the notice), recorded as CONFIRMED and kept as the legal rule by Matthew Wrigley's decision of 28 Oct 2026. Confirm the reading.",
    },
    {
      "before": "Victorian fee notices are generated",
      "key": "NOTICE_MAXIMUM",
      "kind": "LEGAL",
      "point": "Whether a fee notice may give more than 28 days to pay: the approved form says "within 28 days of the date of this notice or by the due date specified below" (guidance only), so NOTICE_RULES records no maximum and refuses every notice until one is checked (a number, or none).",
    },
    {
      "before": "Victorian fee notices are generated",
      "key": "NOTICE_DISPUTE_PROCESS",
      "kind": "STRATAVERZE",
      "point": "The fee notice must describe the dispute resolution process under the owners corporation's rules (s 31(2)(c)). That needs each building's own dispute rule, or the model rule where it has made none (OCR Sch 2 rule 7; DISPUTE_MEETING_PERIOD), which StrataVerze does not hold yet.",
    },
    {
      "before": "Victorian fee notices are generated",
      "key": "NOTICE_DUE_DATE_MOVE",
      "kind": "STRATAVERZE",
      "point": "A fee notice that goes out late falls due 28 days after it is given (StrataVerze's reading of s 31(2)(a)), and each fee notice is given for one instalment (CAV guidance allows one notice with several due dates). Both are StrataVerze choices, not decided. Confirm.",
    },
    {
      "before": "Victorian fee notices are generated",
      "key": "NOTICE_TIER_5",
      "kind": "LEGAL",
      "point": "Tier 5 owners corporations (2-lot subdivisions and services only) are exempt from ss 31 and 32 (ss 7A(1)(a), 8). The exemption is not applied yet: their notices are refused like every other Victorian fee notice.",
    },
    {
      "before": "Victorian fee recovery is offered",
      "key": "FINAL_FEE_NOTICE",
      "kind": "STRATAVERZE",
      "point": "The final fee notice (s 32) is a later PR (stage 2, PR 9). Its rules stay in FEE_NOTICES.finalNotice.",
    },
  ],
